Yes, GitHub natively supports it by default. You do not even have to configure any backend settings on the site; GitHub’s UI automatically looks for a specific file name in your repository. [1, 2, 3, 4]
If you set it up according to GitHub's naming convention, the web interface will completely hide your formatting commits from the blame view. [1, 5]
## How to Make GitHub Honor It

   1. Name the file exactly: Create a file named .git-blame-ignore-revs and place it in the root directory of your repository.
   2. Add the hashes: Add the full 40-character commit hashes of your formatting-only commits to this file, one per line. You can add comments using # to describe the commit.

   # Fix gofmt vertical alignment diff explosion
   7a8b9c0d1e2f3a4b5c6d7e8f9a0b1c2d3e4f5a6b

   3. Commit it: Commit and push this file to your main branch. [1, 2, 4, 6]

Once that file exists on GitHub, any developer who clicks the "Blame" button on the GitHub website will see lines attributed to the actual functional commits, bypassing your formatting commit entirely. [1, 5]
## The Ultimate Workflow to Show Your Team
If your team sets up this file, you can bypass the pipeline restrictions locally while keeping both Git histories clean:

   1. Write your clean logic: Make your 1-line code change.
   2. Commit the logic: git commit -m "feat: add business logic code" (This is your rational, single-line diff).
   3. Run the formatting tool: Run gofmt to satisfy the pipeline, which explodes the 100 lines of whitespace.
   4. Commit the formatting: git commit -m "style: gofmt formatting churn" (This is your formatting-only commit).
   5. Update the ignore file: Copy the hash of your formatting commit, paste it into .git-blame-ignore-revs, and commit that change too.
   6. Push: Your CI pipeline will pass because the code is formatted on the head of your branch, but your code reviewers and future debugging sessions won't have to suffer through the layout noise. [2, 4]

